y of their sum:\n   - If both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are even, \\(a + b\\) is even, and \\(|a - b|\\) is even.\n   - If both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are odd, \\(a + b\\) is even, and \\(|a - b|\\) is even.\n   - If one is even and the other is odd, \\(a + b\\) is odd, and \\(|a - b|\\) is odd.\n5. Since the overall parity of the sum remains unchanged, and we started with an odd sum (1275), the sum of the numbers at any future step will always be odd.\n6. When only one number remains, that number must be the odd cumulative sum of all previous steps, which is still odd.\n\nConclusion: Therefore, the last remaining number is **always odd**.\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The last number is always odd}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( ABCDEF \\) be a convex hexagon where \\( AB \\parallel ED \\), \\( BC \\parallel FE \\), and \\( CD \\parallel AF \\). Prove that \\( R_A + R_C + R_E \\geq \\frac{p}{2} \\), where \\( R_A, R_C, R_E \\) are the radii of the circumcircles of triangles \\( FAB, BCD, \\) and \\( DEF \\) respectively, and \\( p \\) denotes the perimeter of the hexagon.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem about a convex hexagon with some parallel sides, and I need to prove that the sum of the radii of three circumcircles is at least half the perimeter of the hexagon. Hmm. Let me try to parse this step by step.\n\nFirst, the hexagon is labeled ABCDEF. The conditions given are that AB is parallel to ED, BC is parallel to FE, and CD is parallel to AF. So each pair of opposite sides are parallel? Wait, not exactly opposite sides, because in a hexagon, opposite sides would be three apart. Let me check: AB and ED\u2014if you go around the hexagon, A to B to C to D to E to F. So AB is the first side, ED would be the fifth side (from E to D). Wait, but that's not exactly opposite. Opposite sides in a regular hexagon are three apart, like AB and DE. But here, the hexagon is convex but not 