|/2, R_A \u2265 |w|/2, and R_A \u2265 |BF|/2. Similarly for the other triangles.\n\nIf we sum R_A + R_C + R_E, we get:\n\nR_A \u2265 |u|/2, |w|/2, |BF|/2\n\nR_C \u2265 |v|/2, |w|/2, |BD|/2\n\nR_E \u2265 |u|/2, |v|/2, |DF|/2\n\nTherefore, adding them up:\n\nR_A + R_C + R_E \u2265 (|u| + |w| + |v| + |w| + |u| + |v|)/2 = (2|u| + 2|v| + 2|w|)/2 = |u| + |v| + |w| = p/2.\n\nWait, this is exactly the same as before! But this uses only the fact that each circumradius is at least half of each of its triangle's sides. Since in the sum, we take the maximum contributions of each radius to cover the sides |u|, |v|, |w| each twice (since perimeter is 2(|u| + |v| + |w|)). However, in this case, we are using that each radius covers two sides (u and w for R_A, v and w for R_C, u and v for R_E), summing these gives |u| + |v| + |w|, which is p/2.\n\nTherefore, the inequality holds due to the general property of circumradius being at least half of any side of the triangle, combined with the structure of the hexagon having three pairs of parallel sides, which implies it's a zonogon with perimeter twice the sum of the three generating vectors. Therefore, the key idea is that each radius covers two of the generating vectors, and their sum gives p/2.\n\nBut wait, why does this rely on the hexagon being a zonogon? The initial reasoning seems to suggest that it's a general result, but our test with the regular hexagon (a zonogon) works, and the stretched zonogon also works. However, if we consider a non-zonogon convex hexagon with the parallel conditions, would the inequality still hold?\n\nBut the problem specifies the hexagon has parallel sides AB || ED, BC || FE, CD || AF, which makes it a zonogon. Therefore, the structure of the zonogon allows the perimeter to be expressed as twice the sum of three vectors, and the circumradii of the specified triangles each cover two of these vectors, leading to the inequality.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that in a zonogon (convex hexagon with three