(2 sqrt(2}} )) approximately. So, if x_n is slightly above, x_{n+1} is still above but much closer. Wait, this contradicts the earlier alternation idea.\n\nWait, let me clarify. If x_{n-1} > sqrt(2}}, then x_n = (x_{n-1}^2 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} ). Let\u2019s compute x_n - sqrt(2}}:\n\nx_n - sqrt(2}} = (x_{n-1}/2 + 1/x_{n-1}} ) - sqrt(2}}\n\nIf x_{n-1} > sqrt(2}}, then:\n\nx_n - sqrt(2}} = (x_{n-1}/2 - sqrt(2}} ) + 1/x_{n-1}}\n\nBut x_{n-1}/2 - sqrt(2}} = (x_{n-1} - 2 sqrt(2}} ) / 2\n\nSo,\n\nx_n - sqrt(2}} = (x_{n-1} - 2 sqrt(2}} ) / 2 + 1/x_{n-1}}\n\nBut if x_{n-1} > sqrt(2}}, then the sign of x_n - sqrt(2}} depends on the balance between (x_{n-1} - 2 sqrt(2}} ) / 2 and 1/x_{n-1}}.\n\nIf x_{n-1} is very large, (x_{n-1} - 2 sqrt(2}} ) / 2 \u2248 x_{n-1}/2, which is positive, and 1/x_{n-1}} is positive but negligible, so x_n - sqrt(2}} is positive. Hence, x_n > sqrt(2}}.\n\nAs x_{n-1} approaches sqrt(2}} from above, we can expand x_{n-1} = sqrt(2}} + \u03b5 with \u03b5 > 0 small:\n\nx_n - sqrt(2}} = (sqrt(2}} + \u03b5)/2 + 1/(sqrt(2}} + \u03b5) - sqrt(2}}\n\n\u2248 (sqrt(2}} / 2 + \u03b5/2) + (1/sqrt(2}} - \u03b5 / 2 + \u03b5^2 / (2 sqrt(2}} )) - sqrt(2}}\n\n= sqrt(2}} / 2 + \u03b5/2 + 1/sqrt(2}} - \u03b5 / 2 + \u03b5^2 / (2 sqrt(2}} ) - sqrt(2}}\n\n= (sqrt(2}} / 2 + 1/sqrt(2}} ) - sqrt(2}} ) + \u03b5/2 - \u03b5/2 + \u03b5^2 / (2 sqrt(2}} )\n\nNow, sqrt(2}} / 2 + 1/sqrt(2}} ) = sqrt(2}} / 2 + sqrt(2}} / 2 = sqrt(2}}\n\nTherefore, the first terms cancel sqrt(2}}, leaving:\n\nx_n - sqrt(2}} \u2248 \u03b5^2 / (2 sqrt(2}} )\n\nTherefore, when x_{n-1} is slightly above sqrt(2}}, x_n is also above sqrt(2}}, but closer by a factor of \u03b5^2.\n\nTherefore, once the sequence approaches sqrt(2}} from above, it continues to stay above but converges quadratically. Similarly, if it approaches from below, as in x_{n-1} < sqrt(2}}, then x_n is given by:\n\nx_n = (x_{n-1}^2 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} )\n\nIf x_{n-1} < sqrt(2}}, then x_{n-1}^2 < 2, so x_n = (x_{n-1}^2 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} ) > (0 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} ) = 1/x_{n-1}} > 1/sqrt(2}} \u2248 0.707. But this is still below sqrt(2}} only if 1/x_{n-1}} < sqrt(2}}, which requires x_{n-1} > 1/sqrt(2}} \u2248 0.707. So, if x_{n-1} is between 1/sqrt(2}} and sqrt(2}}, then x_n will be greater than sqrt(2}}.\n\nHowever, if x_{n-1} is less than 1/sqrt(2}}, then x_n = (x_{n-1}^2 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} ) = x_{n-1}/2 + 1/x_{n-1}}.\n\nFor x_{n-1} approaching 0, x_n \u2248 1/x_{n-1}}, which tends to infinity.\n\nTherefore, the sequence, when it goes below 1/sqrt(2}}, shoots back up, potentially above sqrt(2}}. However, once it gets close enough to sqrt(2}}, it converges quadratically without oscillating.\n\nBut in our case, starting from x0 = 10^9, the sequence decreases monotonically until it crosses sqrt(2}}, then the next iteration overshoots above sqrt(2}}, and from there, it converges monotonically from above due to the function's convexity. Wait, but earlier analysis suggests that once you are above sqrt(2}}, the iterations decrease monotonically towards sqrt(2}}.\n\nWait, let me verify with x_{n-1} > sqrt(2}}:\n\nCompute x_n - x_{n-1} = (x_{n-1}/2 + 1/x_{n-1}} ) - x_{n-1} = -x_{n-1}/2 + 1/x_{n-1}}.\n\nFor x_{n-1} > sqrt(2}}, we have -x_{n-1}/2 + 1/x_{n-1}} < -sqrt(2}}/2 + 1/sqrt(2}} ) = -sqrt(2}}/2 + sqrt(2}}/2 = 0.\n\nTherefore, x_n < x_{n-1}} if x_{n-1} > sqrt(2}}. So, once above sqrt(2}}, the sequence decreases monotonically towards sqrt(2}}.\n\nSimilarly, if x_{n-1} < sqrt(2}}, then x_n = (x_{n-1}^2 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} ). If x_{n-1} < sqrt(2}}, then x_{n-1}^2 < 2, so x_n = (x_{n-1}^2 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} ) > 2/(2 x_{n-1}} ) = 1/x_{n-1}}.\n\nIf x_{n-1} < 1/sqrt(2}}, then 1/x_{n-1}} > sqrt(2}}, so x_n jumps above sqrt(2}}, and then the sequence continues decreasing monotonically from there.\n\nHowever, if x_{n-1} is between 1/sqrt(2}} and sqrt(2}}, then x_n = (x_{n-1}^2 + 2)/(2 x_{n-1}} ) > sqrt(2}}.\n\nWait, let's take x_{n-1} = 1.4, which is less than sqrt(2}} \u2248 1.4142.\n\nThen, x_n = (1.4^2 + 2)/(2 * 1.4) = (1.96 + 2)/2.8 \u2248 3.96/2.8 \u2248 1.4143, which is greater than sqrt(2}}.\n\nTherefore, even when x_{n-1} is just slightly less than sqrt(2}}, x_n becomes slightly greater than sqrt(2}}, and then the sequence decreases monotonically towards sqrt(2}}.\n\nTherefore, once the sequence enters the interval [1/sqrt(2}}, \u221e), it will converge monotonically to sqrt(2}} from above after one more iteration.\n\nIn our case, starting from x0 = 10^9, the sequence decreases until it reaches x_{30} \u2248 0.9313, which is less than 1/sqrt(2}} \u2248 0.707. Wait, no, 0.9313 is greater than 0.707. 1/sqrt(2}} \u2248 0.7071.\n\nSo x_{30} \u2248 0.9313 is greater than 1/sqrt(2}}, so x_{31} = (0.9313^2 + 2)/(2 * 0.9313} ) \u2248 1.540, which is greater than sqrt(2}}. From there, the sequence decreases monotonically towards sqrt(2}}.\n\nTherefore, after iteration 31, the sequence is above sqrt(2}} and decreases towards it. Therefore, all terms from x_{31} onwards are greater than sqrt(2}}, getting closer each time. Hence, x_{36} would also be greater than sqrt(2}}, making x_{36} - sqrt(2}} positive.\n\nTherefore, combining this with the earlier error bound analysis:\n\n0 < x_{36} - sqrt(2}} < 10^{-9}\n\nHence, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( 0 < x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} < 10^{-9} \\) for the sequence defined by \\( x_0 = 10^9 \\) and \\( x_n = \\frac{x_{n-1}^2 + 2}{2x_{n-1}} \\), we analyze the sequence's behavior through two phases: **contraction** and **quadratic convergence**.\n\n### 1. Contraction Phase:\nThe sequence starts at \\( x_0 = 10^9 \\), which is significantly larger than \\( \\sqrt{2} \\). The recursion \\( x_n = \\frac{x_{n-1}}{2} + \\frac{1}{x_{n-1}} \\) behaves approximately as \\( x_n \\approx \\frac{x_{n-1}}{2} \\) when \\( x_{n-1} \\gg \\sqrt{2} \\). This geometric halving reduces the error exponentially:\n\\[\ne_n = |x_n - \\sqrt{2}| \\approx \\frac{e_{n-1}}{2}.\n\\]\nAfter \\( n \\) iterations, the error is bounded by:\n\\[\ne_n \\leq \\frac{10^9}{2^n}.\n\\]\nTo bring the error below 1, we need \\( n \\geq \\log_2(10^9) \\approx 29.9 \\). Thus, by iteration \\( n = 30 \\), the error is:\n\\[\ne_{30} \\leq \\frac{10^9}{2^{30}} \\approx 0.93.\n\\]\n\n### 2. Quadratic Convergence Phase:\nOnce close to \\( \\sqrt{2} \\), the Newton-Raphson iteration exhibits quadratic convergence. The error satisfies:\n\\[\ne_n \\approx \\frac{e_{n-1}^2}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n\\]\nStarting from \\( e_{30} \\approx 0.93 \\), subsequent errors are:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\ne_{31} &\\approx \\frac{0.93^2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 0.306, \\\\\ne_{32} &\\approx \\frac{0.306^2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 0.033, \\\\\ne_{33} &\\approx \\frac{0.033^2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 0.000388, \\\\\ne_{34} &\\approx \\frac{0.000388^2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 5.32 \\times 10^{-8}, \\\\\ne_{35} &\\approx \\frac{(5.32 \\times 10^{-8})^2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 1.00 \\times 10^{-15}, \\\\\ne_{36} &\\approx \\frac{(1.00 \\times 10^{-15})^2}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 3.54 \\times 10^{-31}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\n### Positivity of \\( x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} \\):\nAfter crossing \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) around iteration 30, the sequence oscillates but remains above \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) starting from iteration 31. Hence, \\( x_{36} > \\sqrt{2} \\), ensuring \\( x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} > 0 \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nBy iteration 36, the error \\( e_{36} \\) is approximately \\( 3.54 \\times 10^{-31} \\), which is indeed less than \\( 10^{-9} \\). Thus:\n\\[\n0 < x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} < 10^{-9}.\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\boxed{0 < x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} < 10^{-9}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's define a sequence \\( \\{ x_n \\} \\) as follows:\n\\( x_0 = 10^9 \\)\n\\( x_n = \\frac{x_{n-1}^2 + 2}{2 x_{n-1}}, \\quad n = 1, 2, \\ldots \\)\n\nWe aim to prove:\n\\[ 0 < x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} < 10^{-9} \\]\n\n1. **Initial Observations**:\n   \n   Let's first note that the function given is a recursive sequence which converges to \\( \\sqrt{2} \\). This convergence can be demonstrated using mathematical induction or fixed-point theorems. For each term \\( x_n \\), we have:\n\n   \\[\n   x_n = \\frac{x_{n-1}^2 + 2}{2 x_{n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Simplifying the Difference**:\n   \n   To simplify \\( x_n - \\sqrt{2} \\), let's rewrite \\( x_n \\) in terms of \\( x_{n-1} \\):\n\n   \\[\n   x_n - \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{x_{n-1}^2 + 2}{2 x_{n-1}} - \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying and dividing by \\( 2x_{n-1} \\) inside the fraction, we get:\n\n   \\[\n   x_n - \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{x_{n-1}^2 + 2 - 2\\sqrt{2} x_{n-1}}{2 x_{n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Factorizing and Bounding**:\n   \n   Recognize that:\n\n   \\[\n   x_n - \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{(x_{n-1} - \\sqrt{2})^2}{2 x_{n-1}}\n   \\]\n   \n   Since \\( x_{n-1} > \\sqrt{2} \\) for all initial positive terms, hence \\( x_{n-1} > \\sqrt{2} \\):\n\n4. **Bounding the Difference**:\n   \n   Since \\( x_{n-1} > \\sqrt{2} \\), we can say:\n\n   \\[\n   x_n - \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{(x_{n-1} - \\sqrt{2})^2}{2 x_{n-1}} < \\frac{(x_{n-1} - \\sqrt{2})^2}{2 \\sqrt{2}}\n   \\]\n\n   We can bound the expression further:\n\n   \\[\n   x_n - \\sqrt{2} < \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{2}} (x_{n-1} - \\sqrt{2})^2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Iterating the Bound**:\n   \n   By repeatedly applying the above inequality, we can bound each subsequent term. It follows that:\n\n   \\[\n   0 < x_n - \\sqrt{2} < \\left( \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\right)^{1 + 2 + ... + 2^{(n-30)}} (x_0 - \\sqrt{2})\n   \\]\n\n6. **Application for \\( n = 36 \\)**:\n   \n   Applying this for \\( n = 36 \\), we sum the exponents up to \\( 36 - 30 = 6 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   0 < x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} < \\left( \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\right)^{63}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Evaluating the Result**:\n   \n   Clearly:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\right)^{63} \\rightarrow This\\text{ } term\\text{ } quickly\\; approaches\\; 0\\; as\\; the\\; exponential\\; decay\\; is\\; significant\\; and\\; much\\; less\\; than\\; 10^{-9}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore:\n\n   \\[\n   0 < x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} < 10^{-9}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThus, the inequality \\( 0 < x_{36} - \\sqrt{2} < 10^{-9} \\) holds true.\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\;} \\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The triangles \\(ABC\\) and \\(AB_1C_1\\) have a common median \\(AM\\). Prove that \\(BC_1 = B_1C\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that BC\u2081 equals B\u2081C in triangles ABC and AB\u2081C\u2081 that share a common median AM. Hmm, let me start by understanding the problem better. \n\nFirst, let's recall what a median is. A median of a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side. Since both triangles ABC and AB\u2081C\u2081 have the common median AM, that means AM must be the median from vertex A in both triangles. So, in triangle ABC, AM is the median to side BC, which implies that M is the midpoint of BC. Similarly, in triangle AB\u2081C\u2081, AM is the median to side B\u2081C\u2081, meaning M is also the midpoint of B\u2081C\u2081. Wait, is that correct? If AM is the median for both triangles, then M has to be the midpoint of BC in triangle ABC and the midpoint of B\u2081C\u2081 in triangle AB\u2081C\u2081. So, M is the midpoint of both BC and B\u2081C\u2081? That seems important.\n\nLet me try to visualize or draw a diagram. Let's have point A at the top, and BC as the base of triangle ABC with M as the midpoint. Then, triangle AB\u2081C\u2081 also has AM as a median, so B\u2081C\u2081 must be another side with M as its midpoint. So, points B\u2081 and C\u2081 must be positioned such that when connected, their midpoint is also M. So, BC and B\u2081C\u2081 are two different segments both having M as their midpoint. That would mean that B\u2081 and C\u2081 are positioned symmetrically with respect to BC, perhaps? Or maybe they are in some other configuration, but both BC and B\u2081C\u2081 share the midpoint M.\n\nSo, the problem states that triangles ABC and AB\u2081C\u2081 share the median AM, which is from 