quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1 \\quad \\text{thus} \\quad 300 = 13 \\cdot 23 + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3y = 1 \\quad (\\text{mod} \\quad 13)\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   3y - 1 \\quad \\text{is divisible by} \\quad 13\n   \\]\n8. To find the values of \\( y \\) such that \\( 3y - 1 \\) is divisible by 13, we check:\n   \\( 3y - 1 = 13k \\)\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{13k + 1}{3}\n   \\]\n   Solving for integer \\( y \\), we systematically test values starting from \\( k = 1 \\):\n   \n   - For \\( k = 1 \\):\n     \\[\n     3y - 1 = 13, \\quad y = \\frac{14}{3} \\quad (\\text{not an integer})\n     \\]\n   \n   - For \\( k = 2 \\):\n     \\[\n     3y - 1 = 26, \\quad y = \\frac{27}{3} = 9\n     \\]\n   \n   - For \\( k = 5 \\):\n     \\[\n     3y - 1 = 65, \\quad y = \\frac{66}{3} = 22\n     \\]\n   \n   For \\( k = 2 \\), \\( y = 9 \\) and for \\( k = 5 \\), \\( y = 22 \\). Check if beyond this yields valid \\( x \\):\n\n   - For \\( y = 22 \\):\n     \\[\n     16y = 16 \\times 22 = 352\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     13x + 352 = 300 \\quad (\\text{not valid since} \\quad 352 \\gt 300)\n     \\]\n     Discarding \\( y = 22 \\) as it results in a negative \\( x \\).\n   \n   Valid calculation:\n   - For \\( y = 9 \\):\n     \\[\n     13x + 16 \\times 9 = 300\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     13x + 144 = 300\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     13x = 156 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = 12\n     \\]\n9. Conclusion: \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{ \\text{There are 12 containers of 130 kg and 9 containers of 160 kg.} }\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nFor a sequence \\( x_{1}, x_{2}, \\ldots, x_{n} \\) of real numbers, we define its price as\n\\[ \\max_{1 \\leqslant i \\leqslant n}\\left|x_{1} + \\cdots + x_{i}\\right|. \\]\n\nGiven \\( n \\) real numbers, Dave and George want to arrange them into a sequence with a low price. Diligent Dave checks all possible ways and finds the minimum possible price \\( D \\). Greedy George,