herefore, numerator becomes:\n\nk/2 * (-2 +3\u221a2 )\n\nSo, x = [ k/2 (-2 +3\u221a2 ) ] / (-7 ) = [ k (-2 +3\u221a2 ) ] / (-14 ) = [ k (2 -3\u221a2 ) ] /14\n\nThus, x = [k (2 -3\u221a2 ) ] /14\n\nNow, recall that y = (k\u00b2)/2 -k x\n\nSubstitute x:\n\ny = (k\u00b2)/2 -k * [k (2 -3\u221a2 ) /14 ] = (k\u00b2)/2 - [k\u00b2 (2 -3\u221a2 ) ] /14\n\nFactor out k\u00b2:\n\n= k\u00b2 [ 1/2 - (2 -3\u221a2 ) /14 ]\n\nCompute the coefficient:\n\n1/2 =7/14\n\nSo:\n\n7/14 - (2 -3\u221a2 )/14 = [7 -2 +3\u221a2 ]/14 = (5 +3\u221a2 )/14\n\nTherefore, y = k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )/14\n\nNow, recall that from equation 1: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 =4y\n\nWe can use this to find k.\n\nWe have expressions for x and y in terms of k. Let's substitute them into x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 =4y.\n\nFirst, compute x\u00b2:\n\nx = [k (2 -3\u221a2 ) ] /14 \u21d2 x\u00b2 = k\u00b2 (2 -3\u221a2 )\u00b2 /196\n\nCompute (2 -3\u221a2 )\u00b2:\n\n=4 -12\u221a2 +9*2=4 -12\u221a2 +18=22 -12\u221a2\n\nSo, x\u00b2 =k\u00b2 (22 -12\u221a2 ) /196\n\nThen, y\u00b2 = [k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )/14 ]\u00b2 =k^4 (5 +3\u221a2 )\u00b2 /196\n\nCompute (5 +3\u221a2 )\u00b2:\n\n=25 +30\u221a2 +9*2=25 +30\u221a2 +18=43 +30\u221a2\n\nThus, y\u00b2 =k^4 (43 +30\u221a2 ) /196\n\nAlso, 4y =4* [k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 ) /14 ]= (4k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )) /14= (2k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )) /7\n\nNow, substitute into x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 =4y:\n\nk\u00b2 (22 -12\u221a2 ) /196 + k^4 (43 +30\u221a2 ) /196 = (2k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )) /7\n\nMultiply all terms by 196 to eliminate denominators:\n\nk\u00b2 (22 -12\u221a2 ) + k^4 (43 +30\u221a2 ) = (2k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )) *28\n\nCompute the right side:\n\n(2k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )) *28 =56k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )\n\nThus, equation becomes:\n\nk\u00b2 (22 -12\u221a2 ) + k^4 (43 +30\u221a2 ) =56k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\nk^4 (43 +30\u221a2 ) +k\u00b2 (22 -12\u221a2 ) -56k\u00b2 (5 +3\u221a2 )=0\n\nFactor out k\u00b2:\n\nk\u00b2 [ k\u00b2 (43 +30\u221a2 ) + (22 -12\u221a2 ) -56(5 +3\u221a2 ) ]=0\n\nSince k \u22600, we have:\n\nk\u00b2 (43 +30\u221a2 ) + (22 -12\u221a2 ) -56(5 +3\u221a2 )=0\n\nCompute the constants:\n\nFirst, expand -56(5 +3\u221a2 )= -280 -168\u221a2\n\nSo, combine constants:\n\n22 -12\u221a2 -280 -168\u221a2 = (22 -280) + (-12\u221a2 -168\u221a2 )= -258 -180\u221a2\n\nTherefore:\n\nk\u00b2 (43 +30\u221a2 ) -258 -180\u221a2 =0 \u21d2 k\u00b2 (43 +30\u221a2 ) =258 +180\u221a2\n\nSolve for k\u00b2:\n\nk\u00b2= (258 +180\u221a2 ) / (43 +30\u221a2 )\n\nSimplify this fraction. Let's rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is (43 -30\u221a2 )\n\nBut first, check if 43 +30\u221a2 and 258 +180\u221a2 have a common factor.\n\nNote that 258 = 43 *6, and 180 =30*6. So:\n\n258 +180\u221a2 =6*(43 +30\u221a2 )\n\nTherefore, k\u00b2= [6*(43 +30\u221a2 ) ] / (43 +30\u221a2 )=6\n\nTherefore, k\u00b2=6 \u21d2k=\u221a6\n\nSince k is the length AC, which is positive, so AC=\u221a6 cm.\n\nWait, that seems elegant. Let me verify:\n\nIf k\u00b2 =6, then the numerator 258 +180\u221a2 is equal to 6*(43 +30\u221a2 ). Let's check:\n\n6*43=258, 6*30\u221a2=180\u221a2. Yes, exactly. Therefore, k\u00b2=6, so k=\u221a6.\n\nTherefore, the length of AC is \u221a6 cm.\n\nLet me just verify this solution to ensure there's no mistake.\n\nGiven AC=\u221a6, then k=\u221a6.\n\nCompute x and y:\n\nx= [k(2 -3\u221a2 ) ] /14 = [\u221a6(2 -3\u221a2 ) ] /14\n\ny= k\u00b2(5 +3\u221a2 ) /14 =6*(5 +3\u221a2 ) /14 = [30 +18\u221a2 ] /14 = [15 +9\u221a2 ] /7\n\nNow, check equation 1: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 =4y\n\nCompute x\u00b2:\n\nx\u00b2= [6(2 -3\u221a2 )\u00b2 ] /14\u00b2 = [6*(4 -12\u221a2 + 9*2 ) ] /196= [6*(4 -12\u221a2 +18 ) ]/196= [6*(22 -12\u221a2 ) ]/196= [132 -72\u221a2 ] /196\n\ny\u00b2= [ (15 +9\u221a2 ) /7 ]\u00b2= [225 +270\u221a2 + 162 ] /49= [387 +270\u221a2 ] /49\n\nCompute x\u00b2 + y\u00b2:\n\n[132 -72\u221a2 ]/196 + [387 +270\u221a2 ] /49 = [132 -72\u221a2 ]/196 + [ (387 +270\u221a2 )*4 ]/196 = [132 -72\u221a2 +1548 +1080\u221a2 ] /196 = (1680 +1008\u221a2 ) /196\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\n1680 \u00f7 28=60, 1008\u221a2 \u00f728=36\u221a2, 196 \u00f728=7. Wait, 1680=28*60, 1008=28*36, 196=28*7\n\nThus:\n\n(28*60 +28*36\u221a2 ) /28*7= [60 +36\u221a2 ] /7\n\n4y=4*(15 +9\u221a2 ) /7= [60 +36\u221a2 ] /7\n\nTherefore, x\u00b2 + y\u00b2=4y. Checked.\n\nSimilarly, check equation 2: (x -k)^2 + (y -3)^2 =9\n\nCompute (x -\u221a6 )\u00b2 + (y -3 )\u00b2:\n\nx -\u221a6= [\u221a6(2 -3\u221a2 ) /14 ] -\u221a6= \u221a6[ (2 -3\u221a2 )/14 -1 ]= \u221a6[ (2 -3\u221a2 -14 ) /14 ]= \u221a6[ (-12 -3\u221a2 ) /14 ]= [ -\u221a6(12 +3\u221a2 ) ] /14\n\nSquare: 6*(144 +72\u221a2 +18 ) /196=6*(162 +72\u221a2 ) /196= (972 +432\u221a2 ) /196= (243 +108\u221a2 ) /49\n\nCompute (y -3 )\u00b2= [ (15 +9\u221a2 ) /7 -3 ]\u00b2= [ (15 +9\u221a2 -21 ) /7 ]\u00b2= [ (-6 +9\u221a2 ) /7 ]\u00b2= (36 -108\u221a2 + 162 ) /49= (198 -108\u221a2 ) /49\n\nSum of both terms:\n\n(243 +108\u221a2 +198 -108\u221a2 ) /49= (441)/49=9. Which matches the radius squared (9). So equation 2 is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, the solution k=\u221a6 is correct. Therefore, the length of AC is \u221a6 cm.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe length of side \\(AC\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{6}}\\) centimeters.", "deepseek_solution": "Given triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle B = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\), we need to find the length of side \\(AC\\). Two circles are involved: one with radius 2 cm passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and tangent to \\(AC\\) at \\(A\\), and another with radius 3 cm passing through \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) and tangent to \\(AC\\) at \\(C\\).\n\n1. **Coordinate System Setup**:\n   - Place \\(A\\) at \\((0, 0)\\) and \\(C\\) at \\((k, 0)\\).\n   - The centers of the circles, \\(O_1\\) and \\(O_2\\), lie on the perpendiculars to \\(AC\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(C\\), respectively. Thus, \\(O_1\\) is at \\((0, 2)\\) and \\(O_2\\) is at \\((k, 3)\\).\n\n2. **Equations for Circles**:\n   - Circle centered at \\(O_1(0, 2)\\) with radius 2: \\(x^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 4\\).\n   - Circle centered at \\(O_2(k, 3)\\) with radius 3: \\((x - k)^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 9\\).\n\n3. **Solving for Coordinates of \\(B\\)**:\n   - Subtract the equations of the circles to find a linear relationship: \\(kx + y = \\frac{k^2}{2}\\).\n   - Substitute \\(y = \\frac{k^2}{2} - kx\\) into the circle equations to find \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(k\\).\n\n4. **Angle Condition**:\n   - Use the dot product to express the angle at \\(B\\): \\(\\cos(30^\\circ) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n   - Simplify the dot product equation using the coordinates of \\(B\\) and the relationships derived.\n\n5. **Solving for \\(k\\)**:\n   - After substituting and simplifying, we find that \\(k^2 = 6\\), leading to \\(k = \\sqrt{6}\\).\n\nThus, the length of side \\(AC\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{6}}\\) centimeters.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the given elements and relevant theorems:**\n\n    In $\\Delta ABC$, $\\angle B = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Two circles are involved:\n    - Circle with center \\( O_{1} \\) and radius \\( R_{1} = 2 \\) cm tangent to \\( AC \\) at point \\( A \\).\n    - Circle with center \\( O_{2} \\) and radius \\( R_{2} = 3 \\) cm tangent to \\( AC \\) at point \\( C \\).\n\n    We need to determine the length of the side \\( AC \\).\n\n2. **Using the theorem about the angle between a tangent and a chord:**\n   \n    - Let \\( \\angle ACB = \\alpha \\) and \\( \\angle BAC = \\beta \\).\n\n    From the tangent-chord angle theorem, we have:\n    \\[\n    \\angle CO_{2}B = 2\\alpha \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 180^\\circ - 2\\alpha\n    \\]\n    and\n    \\[\n    \\angle AO_{1}B = 2\\beta \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 180^\\circ - 2\\beta.\n    \\]\n\n3. **Using the sine rule in the respective segments:**\n   \n    For segment \\( AB \\):\n    \\[\n    AB = 2R_{1} \\cdot \\sin \\beta = 4 \\sin \\beta.\n    \\]\n   \n    For segment \\( BC \\):\n    \\[\n    BC = 2R_{2} \\cdot \\sin \\alpha = 6 \\sin \\alpha.\n    \\]\n\n4. **Applying the Sine Rule in \\( \\Delta ABC \\):**\n\n    According to the Sine Rule:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\alpha} = \\frac{BC}{\\sin \\beta}\n    \\]\n    Substituting the expressions for \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\):\n    \\[\n    \\frac{4 \\sin \\beta}{\\sin \\alpha} = \\frac{6 \\sin \\alpha}{\\sin \\beta}\n    \\]\n     This gives us:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{\\sin^2 \\beta}{\\sin^2 \\alpha} = \\frac{3}{2}.\n    \\]\n\n5. **Relating the angles using the given angle \\( \\angle B = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\):**\n\n    We need the length of \\( AC \\). Using the Sine Rule again, particularly:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AC}{\\sin \\angle ABC} = \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle ACB}\n    \\]\n    Here, using \\( \\angle B = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\):\n    \\[\n    AC = \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\alpha} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n    \\]\n    Substituting \\( AB = 4 \\sin \\beta \\) and \\( \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n    \\[\n    AC = \\frac{4 \\sin \\beta}{\\sin \\alpha} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\sqrt{6}.\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion: \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} \\text{ cm}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Petya has 25 coins, each with a denomination of 1, 2, 5, or 10 rubles. Among these coins, 19 are not two-ruble coins, 20 are not ten-ruble coins, and 16 are not one-ruble coins. How many five-ruble coins does Petya have?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out how many five-ruble coins Petya has. So, Petya has 25 coins total, and each coin can be 1, 2, 5, or 10 rubles. The problem gives us three pieces of information:\n\n1. 19 coins are not two-ruble coins.\n2. 20 coins are not ten-ruble coins.\n3. 16 coins are not one-ruble coins.\n\nHmm, I need to translate these statements into equations. Let's denote the number of 1-ruble coins as A, 2-ruble as B, 5-ruble as C, and 10-ruble as D. So, the total coins equation is A + B + C + D = 25.\n\nNow, the first condition: 19 are not two-ruble coins. That means all coins except the two-ruble ones. So, A + C + D = 19. Because if B is the number of two-ruble coins, then the rest (A, C, D) are not two-ruble coins. So equation 1: A + C + D = 19.\n\nSecond condition: 20 are not ten-ruble coins. That would be all coins except the ten-ruble coins, so A + B + C = 20. So equation 2: A + B + C = 20.\n\nThird condition: 16 are not one-ruble coins. That means all coins except the one-ruble coins, so B + C + D = 16. Equation 3: B + C + D = 16.\n\nSo now we have four equations:\n\n1. A + B + C + D = 25 (total coins)\n2. A + C + D = 19 (not two-ruble)\n3. A + B + C = 20 (not ten-ruble)\n4. B + C + D = 16 (not one-ruble)\n\nLet me see if I can solve these equations step by step. Maybe subtract some equations to find individual variables.\n\nFirst, from equation 1 (total coins), if we subtract equation 2 (A + C + D =19), we get:\n\n(A + B + C + D) - (A + C + D) = 25 - 19 \u21d2 B = 6.\n\nSo, the number of two-ruble coins B is 6. Got that.\n\nNext, let's look at equation 3: B + C + D =16. We already know B is 6, so substitute that in:\n\n6 + C + D =16 \u21d2 C + D =10. Okay, so the total of five and ten-ruble coins is 10.\n\nThen, equation 2: A + B + C =20. We know B is 6, so substitute:\n\nA + 6 + C =20 \u21d2 A + C =14.\n\nFrom equation 1 (total coins): A + B + C + D =25. Again, substitute B=6:\n\nA + 6 + C + D =25 \u21d2 A + C + D =19. Wait, that's equation 2. Hmm, yes, equation 2 is A + C + D =19. So, we have A + C =14 from equation 2 substitution. So if A + C =14 and A + C + D =19, then D =19 -14=5. So D=5.\n\nSince we found earlier that C + D =10, and D=5, then C=10 -5=5. Therefore, C=5. So the number of five-ruble coins is 5?\n\nWait, let's check all variables to make sure.\n\nWe have:\n\nB=6 (two-ruble coins)\n\nD=5 (ten-ruble coins)\n\nC=5 (five-ruble coins)\n\nFrom A + C =14, so A=14 - C=14 -5=9. So A=9 (one-ruble coins)\n\nCheck if all add up to 25:\n\nA + B + C + D =9 +6 +5 +5=25. Correct.\n\nCheck equation 3: B + C + D =6 +5 +5=16. Correct.\n\nEquation 2: A + B + C =9 +6 +5=20. Correct.\n\nEquation 1: A + C + D =9 +5 +5=19. Correct.\n\nEverything checks out. So the answer should be 5 five-ruble coins. Hmm, but let me double-check if there's any misinterpretation.\n\nWait, the problem states \"19 are not two-ruble coins,\" which we took as A + C + D =19, which is correct because those are the coins that are not two-ruble. Similarly, \"20 are not ten-ruble coins\" is A + B + C=20, which is correct. \"16 are not one-ruble coins\" is B + C + D=16, correct. So all equations are set up properly. Solved correctly. So yes, the answer is 5.", "deepseek_solution": "Petya has **5 five-ruble coins**.\n\n---\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Define Variables:**\n   - Let \\( A \\) = number of 1-ruble coins.\n   - Let \\( B \\) = number of 2-ruble coins.\n   - Let \\( C \\) = number of 5-ruble coins.\n   - Let \\( D \\) = number of 10-ruble coins.\n\n2. **Total Coins Equation:**\n   \\[\n   A + B + C + D = 25 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Translate Conditions into Equations:**\n   - \"19 are not two-ruble coins\" \u2192 \\( A + C + D = 19 \\quad \\text{(2)} \\)\n   - \"20 are not ten-ruble coins\" \u2192 \\( A + B + C = 20 \\quad \\text{(3)} \\)\n   - \"16 are not one-ruble coins\" \u2192 \\( B + C + D = 16 \\quad \\text{(4)} \\)\n\n4. **Solve for \\( B \\) (2-ruble coins):**\n   Subtract equation (2) from equation (1):\n   \\[\n   (A + B + C + D) - (A + C + D) = 25 - 19 \\implies B = 6\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve for \\( C + D \\):**\n   Substitute \\( B = 6 \\) into equation (4):\n   \\[\n   6 + C + D = 16 \\implies C + D = 10 \\quad \\text{(5)}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve for \\( A + C \\):**\n   Substitute \\( B = 6 \\) into equation (3):\n   \\[\n   A + 6 + C = 20 \\implies A + C = 14 \\quad \\text{(6)}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Solve for \\( D \\):**\n   Use equation (2) and substitute \\( A + C = 14 \\):\n   \\[\n   14 + D = 19 \\implies D = 5\n   \