m is 10; again 10 isn't prime. (1,8)=9, (2,4)=6. So maybe n=8 can't be partitioned in such a way. So the problem is about n where such a partition exists. So in such n's, the primes must be distinct and not divide n.\n\nAnother example: n=4. Divisors are 1,2,4. Since it's a perfect square, we can't pair them. So n=4 is excluded. Similarly, n=9: divisors 1,3,9. Can't pair them.\n\nSo the problem likely applies to n with an even number of divisors, i.e., non-square n.\n\nBut the problem just says \"positive integer n\" without specifying. So maybe in the case n is a square, the problem statement is vacuously true because you can't partition into pairs. Therefore, the problem probably considers n to have an even number of divisors, i.e., n is not a perfect square.\n\nSo moving forward, assuming n is not a perfect square, so all divisors can be paired as (d, n/d).\n\nNow, each pair sums to a prime. Let me suppose that two different pairs sum to the same prime p. Then we have two different pairs (d1, n/d1) and (d2, n/d2) such that d1 + n/d1 = p and d2 + n/d2 = p.\n\nBut since the divisors are distinct, d1 \u2260 d2 and n/d1 \u2260 n/d2. But maybe they could still sum to the same prime. Let's see. If so, then we have two different equations:\n\nd1 + n/d1 = p,\n\nd2 + n/d2 = p.\n\nBut can two different pairs of divisors lead to the same sum?\n\nTake n=6 again. Divisors 1,2,3,6. The pairs (1,6) sum to 7, and (2,3) sum to 5. Different primes. So in this case, they are distinct. Let's take another example. Suppose n=15. Divisors are 1,3,5,15. Pair (1,15) sum 16, which is not prime. Pair (3,5) sum 8, not prime. So maybe n=15 can't be partitioned. Hmm. Let's take n=10. Divisors 1,2,5,10. Pair (1,10)=11, prime; (2,5)=7, prime. Both primes 11 and 7 are distinct and neither divides 10. So that works. So primes are distinct. So in the examples where such partitions exist, primes are distinct. But how to prove this in general?\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that two different pairs 