 a rhombus.\n\nThus, we conclude that \\(BEDC\\) is a rhombus, and the final answer is \\(\\boxed{BEDC \\text{ is a rhombus}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "Given: In a trapezoid \\(ABCD\\), \\(AB = BC = CD\\). The diagonals intersect at point \\(O\\). The circumcircle of the triangle \\(ABO\\) intersects the base \\(AD\\) at point \\(E\\).\n\n#### 1. Proving the Angles in Trapezoid and Circumcircle\nSince \\(AB = BC = CD\\), \\(ABCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid, and by the properties of an isosceles trapezoid, the angles formed with the diagonals will be equal. Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\angle BDC &= \\angle DBC \\\\\n\\intertext{Furthermore, because \\(\\angle BC = \\angle CAD = \\angle DBC\\), we can write:}\n\\angle DBC &= \\angle DBC = \\angle CAB = \\angle CAD\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSince angles \\(OAE\\) and \\(OBE\\) subtend the same arc in the circumcircle, the angles are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\angle CAD &= \\angle OAE = \\angle OBE = \\angle DBE\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\n#### 2. Proving \\(BE \\parallel CD\\)\nSince \\( \\angle BDC = \\angle DBE \\), it implies that\n\n\\[\nBE \\parallel CD\n\\]\n\n#### 3. Showing \\(BECD\\) is a Parallelogram and a Rhombus\nBecause \\(BE \\parallel CD\\), quadrilateral \\(BECD\\) is a parallelogram. Given that \\(AB = BC = CD\\), all adjacent sides of the parallelogram are equal:\n\n\\[\nBE = AB = BC = CD\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(BECD\\) is a parallelogram with equal sides, implying that it is a rhombus. By definition, a rhombus is a parallelogram with all sides equal.\n\nThus we can conclude:\n\\(\\boxed{BECD\\text{ is a rhombus}}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Show that the exponential generating function \\( E_{P}(x) = \\sum_{N=0}^{\\infty} P_{N} \\frac{x^{N}}{N!} \\) of the sequence \\( P = \\left(P_{N}\\right)_{N \\geqslant 0} \\) (where the numbers \\( P_{N}, N \\geqslant 0 \\), are defined in problem I.2.16) is given by the follow