r (and hence concurrent), the locus is the entire three-dimensional space.\n\nThus, the final answer for part (a) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All of space}}\n\\]\n\nb) When considering triangles \\( ABC \\) whose planes are parallel to a given plane, the locus of their centroids is constrained. For non-coplanar lines (concurrent with non-coplanar direction vectors), this locus forms a line. The direction of this line is determined by the normal vector of the given plane and the direction vectors of the lines.\n\nThus, the final answer for part (b) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A line}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### Part (a)\n\nTo identify the geometric location of the centers of gravity of the triangles \\(ABC\\) with vertices on three pairwise intersecting lines:\n\n1. Suppose the three pairwise intersecting lines \\(l_1\\), \\(l_2\\), and \\(l_3\\) exist in space.\n   \n2. Any triangle \\(ABC\\) formed by selecting points \\(A, B, C\\) on \\(l_1, l_2,\\) and \\(l_3\\) respectively will have its centroid \\(G\\) located at:\n   \\[\n   G = \\left(\\frac{x_A + x_B + x_C}{3}, \\frac{y_A + y_B + y_C}{3}, \\frac{z_A + z_B + z_C}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n   where \\(A = (x_A, y_A, z_A)\\), \\(B = (x_B, y_B, z_B)\\), and \\(C = (x_C, y_C, z_C)\\).\n\n3. As \\(A, B, C\\) freely move along their respective lines, the centroid \\(G\\) can cover the entire space.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The geometric location of the centers of gravity of the triangles is the entire space.}}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (b)\n\nTo identify the geometric location of the centers of gravity of the triangles \\(ABC\\) with vertices on three pairwise intersecting lines, with planes parallel to a given plane:\n\n1. Suppose the three pairwise intersecting lines \\(l_1\\), \\(l_2\\), and \\(l_3\\) exist in planes parallel to a given plane \\(\\pi\\).\n\n2. When we take the intersections along these planes, each triangle \\(ABC\\) formed by selecting points \\(A, B, C\\) on \\(l_1, l_2, l_3\\) respectively will still have its centroid \\(G\\):\n   \\[\n   G = \\left(\\frac{x_A + x_B + x_C}{3}, \\frac{y_A + y_B + y_C}{3}, \\frac{z_A + z_B + z_C}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. Given that the planes are parallel to \\(\\pi\\), all points \\(A, B, C\\) maintain a fixed distance from \\(\\pi\\) in the direction normal to \\(\\pi\\).\n\n4. Applying the calculation steps demonstrated in problem 266, we can deduce that since the triangles are on parallel planes, the line segments within the dimensions parallel to \\(\\pi\\) freely vary while the distances perpendicular to \\(\\pi\\) remain constant.\n\n5. Thus, the geometric location of the centers of gravity for all such formed triangles will align.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The geometric location of the centers of gravity of these triangles is a line.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A parabola $\\Pi_{1}$ with its branches facing upwards passes through the points $(10,0)$ and $(13,0)$. Another parabola $\\Pi_{2}$, also with its branches facing upwards, passes through the point $(13,0)$. It is also known that the vertex of the parabola $\\Pi_{1}$ bisects the segment connecting the origin and the vertex of the parabola $\\Pi_{2}$. What is the x-coordinate of the other point where the parabola $\\Pi_{2}$ intersects the $x$-axis?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem about two parabolas, \u03a0\u2081 and \u03a0\u2082. Let me try to parse all the information given step by step.\n\nFirst, \u03a0\u2081 is a parabola opening upwards, and it passes through the points (10,0) and (13,0). So, those are the x-intercepts of \u03a0\u2081. Since it's a parabola opening upwards, its equation should be in the form y = a(x - h)\u00b2 + k, where (h, k) is the vertex. But since it has x-intercepts at 10 and 13, another way to write its equation is y = a(x - 10)(x - 13). That makes sense because when x is 10 or 13, y is 0. Then the vertex wo