58)^2 + (-0.5)^2 )\u2248sqrt(0.0073 +0.25)=sqrt(0.2573)\u22480.5073.\n\nSo triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081 has sides approximately: 0.5858, 0.5073, 0.5073. So it's an isosceles triangle with two sides equal. Therefore, angles at B\u2081 and C\u2081 are equal, and angle at A\u2081 is different.\n\nNow, we need to check if AA\u2081 is the angle bisector of angle A\u2081 in triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081.\n\nFirst, find the angle bisector of angle A\u2081. The angle bisector theorem states that it divides the opposite side (B\u2081C\u2081) into segments proportional to the adjacent sides.\n\nIn triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081, angle bisector from A\u2081 should divide side B\u2081C\u2081 into ratio A\u2081B\u2081 / A\u2081C\u2081.\n\nCompute lengths:\n\nA\u2081B\u2081\u22480.5858, A\u2081C\u2081\u22480.5073.\n\nRatio\u22480.5858 /0.5073\u22481.155.\n\nSo the angle bisector from A\u2081 should divide B\u2081C\u2081 into segments proportional to ~1.155.\n\nBut in reality, line AA\u2081 goes from A\u2081 to A(0,1). Let's find where AA\u2081 intersects B\u2081C\u2081. Wait, but A is not part of triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081. Wait, AA\u2081 is the line from A to A\u2081, which is a vertex of triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081. Wait, triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081 has vertices at A\u2081, B\u2081, C\u2081, so point A is outside this triangle.\n\nWait, but the problem states that in triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081, the lines AA\u2081, BB\u2081, and CC\u2081 are also angle bisectors. But AA\u2081 is a line from A to A\u2081, where A is outside triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081. However, angle bisectors are lines from the vertices of the triangle. So if AA\u2081 is an angle bisector of triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081, then A must be a vertex of triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081. But triangle A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081 has vertices A\u2081, B\u2081, C\u2081. Therefore, this is impossible unless A coincides with one of the vertices of A\u2081B\u2081C\u2081, which it doesn't unless ABC is degenerat