n- **Italian**: \\(\\frac{280}{4} = 70\\)\n\nSumming these:  \n\\(56 + 40 + 70 = 166\\)\n\n**Spanish books**:  \n\\(280 - 166 = 114\\)\n\n**Answer**: The library contains \\(\\boxed{114}\\) Spanish books.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Determine the total number of books:**\n\n   We know Jo\u00e3o's father's library contains between 200 and 300 books. We also know some specific fractions of the total books. Specifically:\n   - \\(\\frac{1}{5}\\) of the books are in English,\n   - \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) of the books are in French,\n   - \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) of the books are in Italian.\n\n   Since these fractions must add to the books in those languages and the rest will be in Spanish, let's proceed by identifying a common multiple of the denominators (5, 7, and 4) in the given range.\n\n2. **Calculate the least common multiple (LCM):**\n\n   \\( \\text{LCM}(5, 7, 4) \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 5 \\times 7 \\times 4 = 140\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, 140, being the LCM, must fit into the range [200, 300] as asked. Thus, the multiple of 140 in this range is:\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 2 = 280\n   \\]\n   Hence, the only possible value of books in the library from 200 to 300 that fits all criteria is \\(280\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the number of books in various languages:**\n\n   - Books in English:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{5} \\times 280 = 56\n   \\]\n   - Books in French:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{7} \\times 280 = 40\n   \\]\n   - Books in Italian:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{4} \\times 280 = 70\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sum the books that are not in Spanish:**\n\n   \\[\n   56 + 40 + 70 = 166\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the number of books in Spanish:**\n\n   Subtract the total non-Spanish books from the total books:\n   \\[\n   280 - 166 = 114\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{114}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "(Helly's theorem in dimension 2) Consider four convex sets in the plane such that the intersection of any 