:\n\na) The intersection is non-empty as shown.\n\nb) Yes, the theorem remains true for n \u2265 4.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\na) \\boxed{\\text{The intersection of all four convex sets is non-empty.}}\n\nb) \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}", "deepseek_solution": "To address part (a), we need to show that the intersection of all four convex sets in the plane is non-empty given that the intersection of any three is non-empty. We start by picking a point from each triple intersection:\n\n- \\( p_1 \\in C_2 \\cap C_3 \\cap C_4 \\)\n- \\( p_2 \\in C_1 \\cap C_3 \\cap C_4 \\)\n- \\( p_3 \\in C_1 \\cap C_2 \\cap C_4 \\)\n- \\( p_4 \\in C_1 \\cap C_2 \\cap C_3 \\)\n\nBy Radon's theorem, any four points in the plane can be partitioned into two subsets whose convex hulls intersect. Consider such a partition \\( A \\) and \\( B \\). Without loss of generality, suppose \\( A = \\{p_1, p_2\\} \\) and \\( B = \\{p_3, p_4\\} \\). The convex hulls \\( \\text{conv}(A) \\) and \\( \\text{conv}(B) \\) intersect at some point \\( x \\).\n\nSince \\( p_1 \\) and \\( p_2 \\) are in \\( C_3 \\) and \\( C_4 \\), any convex combination of \\( p_1 \\) and \\( p_2 \\) (i.e., \\( \\text{conv}(A) \\)) is in \\( C_3 \\) and \\( C_4 \\). Similarly, \\( p_3 \\) and \\( p_4 \\) are in \\( C_1 \\) and \\( C_2 \\), so any convex combination of \\( p_3 \\) and \\( p_4 \\) (i.e., \\( \\text{conv}(B) \\)) is in \\( C_1 \\) and \\( C_2 \\). Therefore, the intersection point \\( x \\) must be in all four sets \\( C_1, C_2, C_3, \\) and \\( C_4 \\), proving the intersection is non-empty.\n\nFor part (b), Helly's theorem in dimension 2 states that for any finite collection of convex sets in the plane, if the intersection of every three sets is non-empty, then the intersection of all sets is non-empty. This holds for any number of sets \\( n \\geq 3 \\). Therefore, replacing 4 with \\( n \\geq 4 \\) still satisfies the conditions of Helly's theorem, ensuring the intersection remains non-empty.\n\n### Final Answer\n\na) \\boxed{\\text{The intersection of all four convex sets is non-empty.}}\n\nb) \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### Part (a):\n\n**Statement:**\nShow that if four convex sets \\(C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4\\) in the plane have the property that any three of them intersect, then all four sets have a non-empty intersection.\n\n**Proof:**\n\n1. Let's denote the four convex sets by \\(C_1, C_2, C_3,\\) and \\(C_4\\).\n\n2. According to the hypothesis, for each integer \\(i\\) from 1 to 4, there exists a point \\(A_i\\) that belongs to the intersection of the other three convex sets. This implies:\n   \\[\n   A_1 \\in C_2 \\cap C_3 \\cap C_4, \\quad A_2 \\in C_1 \\cap C_3 \\cap C_4, \\quad A_3 \\in C_1 \\cap C_2 \\cap C_4, \\quad A_4 \\in C_1 \\cap C_2 \\cap C_3.\n   \\]\n\n3. Now, consider the positions of \\(A_1, A_2, A_3,\\) and \\(A_4\\):\n   \n   - **Case 1**: The points \\(A_1, A_2, A_3, A_4\\) form a convex quadrilateral.\n     - In this case, without loss of generality, assume that the line segments \\(\\left[A_1 A_2\\right]\\) and \\(\\left[A_3 A_4\\right]\\) intersect at a point \\(X\\).\n     - Since \\(A_1\\) and \\(A_2\\) belong to \\(C_3\\) and \\(C_4\\) and these sets are convex, the convex combination (in particular the line segment \\(\\left[A_1 A_2\\right]\\)) is also within \\(C_3\\) and \\(C_4\\).\n     - Similarly, since \\(A_3\\) and \\(A_4\\) belong to \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\), the line segment \\(\\left[A_3 A_4\\right]\\) is inside \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\).\n     - Hence, the intersection point \\(X\\) of the two segments \\(\\left[A_1 A_2\\right]\\) and \\(\\left[A_3 A_4\\right]\\), belonging to all \\(C_1, C_2, C_3,\\) and \\(C_4\\), verifies that \\(X \\in C_1 \\cap C_2 \\cap C_3 \\cap C_4\\).\n\n   - **Case 2**: One of the points \\(A_i\\) lies inside the triangle formed by the other three points.\n     - Without loss of generality, assume \\(A_1\\) is inside the triangle \\(\\Delta A_2 A_3 A_4\\).\n     - Since \\(A_2, A_3,\\) and \\(A_4\\) belong to \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_1\\) is convex, \\(A_1\\) (being inside \\(\\Delta A_2 A_3 A_4\\)) implies that \\(A_1 \\in C_1\\).\n     - Given \\(A_1 \\in C_2 \\cap C_3 \\cap C_4\\) by the hypothesis, it follows that \\(A_1\\) belongs to \\(C_1\\) as well.\n     - Hence, \\(A_1 \\in C_1 \\cap C_2 \\cap C_3 \\cap C_4\\).\n\nThus, both cases ensure the intersection of all four convex sets is non-empty.\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{The intersection of the four convex sets is non-empty.}}\\)\n\n#### Part (b):\n\n**Statement:**\nDetermine whether the theorem remains true when replacing 4 with \\(n \\geq 4\\).\n\n**Proof:**\n\n1. **Base Case (n=4):** This is established in part (a) where we proved that the intersection of four convex sets is non-empty when any three of them intersect.\n\n2. **Induction Hypothesis:** Assume the theorem is valid for any \\(n\\) convex sets; i.e., if any \\(n-1\\) convex sets intersect, then all \\(n\\) sets intersect.\n\n3. **Inductive Step:** Show that the theorem holds for \\(n+1\\) convex sets \\(C_1, C_2, \\ldots, C_{n+1}\\):\n   - Given the condition that any \\(n\\) of these \\(n+1\\) sets intersect.\n   - For each \\(i\\) from 1 to \\(n+1\\), let \\(A_i\\) be a point in \\(\\bigcap_{j \\neq i} C_j\\). This is possible by the induction hypothesis.\n\n4. By using the same logic as for \\(n=4\\):\n   - If the points \\(A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_{n+1}\\) are in general convex configuration, the intersection point will lie in all \\(C_i\\)'s by the convexity argument.\n   - If one point lies inside the convex hull of the others (like being inside a higher-dimensional simplex), then by the convexity, the same argument shows it lies in all sets.\n\nHence, by induction, the theorem holds for any \\(n \\geq 4\\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes, the theorem holds for any \\( n \\geq 4\\).}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In $\\triangle ABC$, if $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|=2$, $|\\overrightar