egrees (since partitions have different sizes, hence different degrees), so when vertices from different partitions are adjacent, their degrees are different. Therefore, this graph satisfies the condition, even though multiple vertices share the same degree, as long as those with the same degree are not adjacent.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the number of edges is 870, which seems to be the maximum possible under these constraints.\n\nBut is there a way to get more edges? For example, could we have a graph where some vertices have the same degree but are not adjacent, and other vertices have unique degrees and can be connected freely?\n\nSuppose we have some partitions with the same degree but placed in different classes (so they are not adjacent), and other partitions with unique degrees. This might allow more edges.\n\nFor example, suppose we have two partitions with degree 40, but they are placed in different classes (independent sets), so they are not adjacent to each other but can be adjacent to other partitions. Then, other partitions have unique degrees. However, this complicates the structure.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we have a graph where as many degrees as possible are unique, and the remaining degrees are grouped into partitions where same-degree vertices are not adjacent. This might allow higher edges.\n\nBut this approach seems vague. Let's think differently.\n\nWhat is the maximum number of edges in a graph of 45 vertices where no two adjacent vertices have the same degree? This is equivalent to a proper edge-coloring, but here it's a constraint on degrees rather than colors. I recall that such graphs are called \"irregular graphs,\" but irregular graphs typically have all vertices with distinct degrees, which is a stronger condition.\n\nWait, if all degrees are distinct, then it's an irregular graph, which automatically satisfies the condition because adjacent vertices have different degrees. However, irregular graphs are very rare and exist only for 