that \\(CM = BC\\) and \\(MD = AD\\), as required.\n\nBut let me check if there's a simpler way without coordinates. Since the problem mentions that there exists a unique point \\(M\\) such that \\(\\angle BMA = 90^\\circ\\), which suggests that \\(M\\) lies on the circle with diameter \\(AB\\). Wait, no, the circle we considered earlier was centered at \\((0, h/2)\\) with radius \\(h/2\\), which is actually the circle with diameter from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((0, h)\\), which is the vertical line segment \\(AB\\). Wait, no. The circle centered at \\((0, h/2)\\) with radius \\(h/2\\) is the set of points \\(M\\) such that \\(x^2 + (y - h/2)^2 = (h/2)^2\\). This is a circle with center at the midpoint of \\(AB\\) (since \\(AB\\) is vertical from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((0, h)\\)) and radius \\(h/2\\), so it's the circle with diameter \\(AB\\). Wait, but diameter \\(AB\\) is vertical, so the circle would have all points at distance \\(h/2\\) from the center \\((0, h/2)\\), which is indeed the case. So points on this circle satisfy \\(\\angle AMB = 90^\\circ\\), but actually, according to the circle, any point on the circle would form a right angle with the diameter endpoints. But in our case, the circle has diameter \\(AB\\), so any point \\(M\\) on the circle satisfies \\(\\angle AMB = 90^\\circ\\). Wait, but in the problem, it's \\(\\angle BMA = 90^\\circ\\), which is the same as \\(\\angle AMB = 90^\\circ\\). Therefore, the circle with diameter \\(AB\\) is indeed the locus of points \\(M\\) such that \\(\\angle AMB = 90^\\circ\\). Therefore, the intersection of this circle with line \\(CD\\) gives the possible positions of \\(M\\). Since it's given that there's a unique such \\(M\\), line \\(CD\\) must be tangent to this circle, hence the previous condition \\(h^2 = 4ab\\). \n\nGiven that \\(h^2 = 4ab\\), then \\(h = 2\\sqrt{ab}\\). Therefore, the height of the trapezoid is twice the geometric mean of the lengths of the two bases \\(AD = a\\) and \\(BC = b\\). With this condi