n:\n\n\\[\n(2x - 3)^2 = \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{18}{x} + 1\\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{18}{x} + 1\\right) = \\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\cdot \\frac{18}{x}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\cdot 1\\right) = 9 + \\frac{x}{2}\n\\]\n\nExpanding the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n(2x - 3)^2 = 4x^2 - 12x + 9\n\\]\n\nEquating both sides:\n\n\\[\n4x^2 - 12x + 9 = 9 + \\frac{x}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 9 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n4x^2 - 12x = \\frac{x}{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n8x^2 - 24x = x\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[\n8x^2 - 25x = 0\n\\]\n\nFactoring out \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx(8x - 25) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives solutions \\(x = 0\\) or \\(x = \\frac{25}{8}\\). Since \\(x = 0\\) is invalid (it makes terms undefined), we use \\(x = \\frac{25}{8}\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(x = \\frac{25}{8}\\) into the terms:\n\n- First term: \\(\\frac{x}{2} = \\frac{25}{16}\\)\n- Second term: \\(2x - 3 = \\frac{13}{4}\\)\n- Third term: \\(\\frac{18}{x} + 1 = \\frac{169}{25}\\)\n\nCalculating the common ratio:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Common ratio} = \\frac{\\frac{13}{4}}{\\frac{25}{16}} = \\frac{13}{4} \\cdot \\frac{16}{25} = \\frac{52}{25} = 2.08\n\\]\n\nVerifying the ratio between the third and second term confirms the same result. Thus, the common ratio is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2.08}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let us consider the numbers given to form a geometric progression:\n   \\[\n   a_1 = \\frac{x}{2}, \\quad a_2 = 2x - 3, \\quad a_3 = \\frac{18}{x} + 1\n   \\]\n   Since these numbers form a geometric progression, they must satisfy the characteristic property:\n   \\[\n   a_n \\cdot a_{n+2} = a_{n+1}^2\n   \\]\n   For our specific sequence, this translates to:\n   \\[\n   a_1 \\cdot a_3 = a_2^2\n   \\]\n   \n2. Substitute the given values into the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{18}{x} + 1 \\right) = (2x - 3)^2\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the left side of the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{18}{x} + 1 \\right) = \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot \\frac{18}{x} + \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{x \\cdot 18}{2x} + \\frac{x}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 9 + \\frac{x}{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. Write the equation:\n   \\[\n   9 + \\frac{x}{2} = (2x - 3)^2\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify and set the equation to zero:\n   \\[\n   9 + \\frac{x}{2} = 4x^2 - 12x + 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9 + \\frac{x}{2} - 4x^2 + 12x - 9 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{2} - 4x^2 + 12x = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. Clear the fraction by multiplying all terms by 2:\n   \\[\n   x - 8x^2 + 24x = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -8x^2 + 25x = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. Factor the quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   x(8x - 25) = 0\n   \\]\n\n8. Solve for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   x = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 8x - 25 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8x - 25 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{25}{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(x = 0\\) would violate the conditions (denominator cannot be zero), we take:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{25}{8}\n   \\]\n\n9. Now, substitute \\(x = \\frac{25}{8}\\) back to find the first term in the geometric progression:\n   \\[\n   a_1 = \\frac{x}{2} = \\frac{ \\frac{25}{8}}{2} = \\frac{25}{16}\n   \\]\n\n10. Calculate the common ratio \\(q\\):\n    \\[\n    q = \\frac{a_2}{a_1} = \\frac{2x - 3}{\\frac{x}{2}}\n    \\]\n    Substitute \\(x = \\frac{25}{8}\\):\n    \\[\n    a_2 = 2x - 3 = 2 \\cdot \\frac{25}{8} - 3 = \\frac{50}{8} - 3 = \\frac{50}{8} - \\frac{24}{8} = \\frac{26}{8} = \\frac{13}{4} \n    \\]\n    Therefore:\n    \\[\n    q = \\frac{ \\frac{13}{4}}{\\frac{25}{16}} = \\frac{13}{4} \\cdot \\frac{16}{25} = \\frac{13 \\cdot 4}{25} = \\frac{52}{25}\n    \\]\n\n11. Conclude with the calculated common ratio:\n    \\[\n    q = \\frac{52}{25} = 2.08\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe common ratio of the geometric progression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\frac{52}{25} = 2.08 }\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Through point 