efore, 4x +1 is the minimum.\n\nSecond interval, 1/3 \u2264x <1/2: At x=1/3, 4x +1 and x +2 are equal. For x slightly larger than 1/3, since 4x +1 increases faster, x +2 will be smaller. Let me confirm with x=0.4 (which is in 1/3\u22480.333 to 0.5). At x=0.4:\n\n4*0.4 +1 = 1.6 +1 = 2.6  \n0.4 +2 = 2.4  \n-2*0.4 +4 = 3.2\n\nSo minimum is 2.4 (x +2). So in this interval, x +2 is the minimum.\n\nThird interval, 1/2 \u2264x <2/3. Let's pick x=0.6 (which is 3/5, 0.6 is between 0.5 and 0.666...).\n\nCompute each function:\n\n4*0.6 +1 = 2.4 +1 = 3.4  \n0.6 +2 = 2.6  \n-2*0.6 +4 = -1.2 +4 = 2.8\n\nSo the minimum here is 2.6 from x +2. Wait, but wait: is x +2 still the minimum here? Let me check another point, say x=0.55.\n\n4*0.55 +1 = 2.2 +1 = 3.2  \n0.55 +2 = 2.55  \n-2*0.55 +4 = -1.1 +4 = 2.9\n\nMinimum is 2.55 (x +2). Hmm. What about closer to x=2/3, say x=0.65.\n\n4*0.65 +1 = 2.6 +1 = 3.6  \n0.65 +2 = 2.65  \n-2*0.65 +4 = -1.3 +4 = 2.7\n\nMinimum is 2.65 (x +2). Wait, but at x=2/3, x +2 and -2x +4 meet. So at x=2/3, x +2 = 2/3 + 6/3 = 8/3 \u22482.666, and -2x +4 = -4/3 +12/3=8/3. So at x=2/3, both are equal. Then, beyond x=2/3, which function is lower?\n\nWait, let's check the fourth interval: x \u22652/3. Let's take x=1.\n\n4*1 +1=5  \n1 +2=3  \n-2*1 +4=2\n\nSo the minimum is 2, from -2x +4. Wait, but at x=1, -2x +4=2, which is lower than x +2=3 and 4x +1=5. So in the interval x \u22652/3, the minimum is -2x +4. Let's check x=0.7 (which is greater than 2/3\u22480.666):\n\n4*0.7 +1=2.8 +1=3.8  \n0.7 +2=2.7  \n-2*0.7 +4= -1.4 +4=2.6\n\nSo minimum is 2.6 from -2x +4. Wait, but 2.6 is less than 2.7. So actually, at x=0.7, -2x +4=2.6 is the minimum, which is lower than x +2=2.7. So in the interval x \u22652/3, the minimum is -2x +4. Therefore, in the third interval (1/2 \u2264x <2/3), the minimum is x +2 until x=2/3 where it becomes -2x +4. Wait, but when we checked x=0.6 (which is 3/5=0.6 <2/3\u22480.666), the minimum was x +2=2.6, but at x=2/3, x +2 and -2x +4 are equal.