n at x=1/2 between 4x +1 and -2x +4 might not be a point where the minimum switches, because the third function, x +2, is still lower there. Let me verify.\n\nAt x=1/2, we have:\n\n4x +1 = 3  \nx +2 = 2.5  \n-2x +4 = 3\n\nSo the minimum is 2.5 from x +2. Therefore, even though 4x +1 crosses -2x +4 at x=1/2, since x +2 is still lower there, the minimum doesn't switch. Therefore, the only points where the minimum switches are at x=1/3 (from 4x +1 to x +2) and at x=2/3 (from x +2 to -2x +4). Therefore, the intervals where the minimum is determined by different functions are:\n\n1. x <1/3: min is 4x +1\n2. 1/3 \u2264x <2/3: min is x +2\n3. x \u22652/3: min is -2x +4\n\nHence, the function f(x) is piecewise linear, increasing from x approaching -infty up to x=2/3, and then decreasing after that. Wait, no. Wait, 4x +1 is increasing, x +2 is increasing, and -2x +4 is decreasing. So in the first interval, f(x) =4x +1, which is increasing. Then, from x=1/3 to x=2/3, f(x) =x +2, still increasing. Then, after x=2/3, f(x)=-2x +4, which is decreasing. Therefore, the maximum value of f(x) must occur at x=2/3, where the function switches from increasing to decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of f(x) is at x=2/3, which is f(2/3)=8/3\u22482.666...\n\nWait, but let's check. At x=2/3, f(x)=x +2=8/3\u22482.666..., and also f(x)=-2x +4=8/3. Since both are equal here. But just to the left of x=2/3, say x=0.66, x +2=0.66 +2=2.66, and -2x +4= -1.32 +4=2.68, so x +2 is still lower. Just to the right, x=0.67, x +2=2.67, -2x +4\u2248-1.34 +4=2.66, so now -2x +4 is lower. So at x=2/3, the switch happens. Therefore, the maximum of f(x) is at x=2/3, where f(x)=8/3.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm by checking the behavior:\n\n- For x <1/3, f(x)=4x +1, increasing.\n- For 1/3 \u2264x <2/3, f(x)=x +2, also increasing.\n- For x \u22652/3, f(x)=-2x +4, decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the function f(x) increases up to x=2/3 and then decreases. Therefore, the maximum is at x=2/3, which is 8/3.\n\nBut let 