\), the minimum function is \\( x + 2 \\), which is increasing.\n   - For \\( x \\geq \\frac{2}{3} \\), the minimum function is \\( -2x + 4 \\), which is decreasing.\n\n3. **Behavior at Critical Points**:\n   - At \\( x = \\frac{1}{3} \\), \\( f(x) = \\frac{7}{3} \\).\n   - At \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\), \\( f(x) = \\frac{5}{2} \\).\n   - At \\( x = \\frac{2}{3} \\), \\( f(x) = \\frac{8}{3} \\).\n\nSince \\( f(x) \\) increases up to \\( x = \\frac{2}{3} \\) and then decreases, the maximum value of \\( f(x) \\) occurs at \\( x = \\frac{2}{3} \\).\n\nThus, the maximum value of \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{8}{3}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Identify the Functions:**\n   - The given functions are \\(4x + 1\\), \\(x + 2\\), and \\(-2x + 4\\).\n\n2. **Find Intersection Points:**\n   - To find the minimum value, consider where these functions intersect.\n   \n3. **Calculate Intersection Points:**\n\n   - **Intersection of \\(4x + 1\\) and \\(-2x + 4\\):**\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n         4x + 1 &= -2x + 4 \\\\\n         4x + 2x &= 4 - 1 \\\\\n         6x &= 3 \\\\\n         x &= \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) back into either equation to find \\(y\\):\n     \\[\n     y = 4 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 3\n     \\]\n     So, \\(M_2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 3\\right)\\).\n\n   - **Intersection of \\(x + 2\\) and \\(-2x + 4\\):**\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n         x + 2 &= -2x + 4 \\\\\n         x + 2x &= 4 - 2 \\\\\n         3x &= 2 \\\\\n         x &= \\frac{2}{3}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\(x = \\frac{2}{3}\\) back into either equation to find \\(y\\):\n     \\[\n     y = \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) + 2 = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{6}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n     \\]\n     So, \\(M_3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\).\n\n   - **Intersection of \\(4x + 1\\) and \\(x + 2\\):**\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n         4x + 1 &= x + 2 \\\\\n         4x - x &= 2 - 1 \